From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Pfeiffer <cpfeiffer@rev-crew.info>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 02:43:51 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Fix SDK Hooks growing the game's entity listeners with
 the wrong heap

SDK Hooks adds itself to the game's entity listeners,
CGlobalEntityList's CUtlVector<IEntityListener *>, with AddToTail.
CUtlMemory grows with plain malloc and realloc. The game's libraries
route those to tier0 (memoverride.cpp), but SourceMod's extensions
don't link it, so a growth in the extension comes from the C runtime's
heap. The vector's block is then the C runtime's, and the game hands it
to tier0's realloc the next time it grows the vector, on any
architecture. Whether that goes wrong depends on the game's tier0.

When SDK Hooks loads at the server's start, i.e. a plugin loaded then
uses it, the vector is always empty: the games add their listeners in
LevelInitPreEntity, at the map start. SDK Hooks makes the first
allocation, which CUtlMemory makes 32 bytes whatever the size of the
elements: 8 slots on x86, 4 on x86_64. The game then adds its own,
measured until the first map had started, with stock SourceMod (master,
cfcda295) and a plugin that only includes <sdkhooks>, under a debugger
with write breakpoints on the vector:

  Game               Listeners  x86: grown?  x86_64: grown?
  Day of Infamy      6          no (8)       yes, crashes in tier0
  Insurgency         6          no (8)       yes, crashes in tier0
  Team Fortress 2    6          no (8)       yes, no crash
  Counter-Strike: S  6          no (8)       yes, no crash
  Day of Defeat: S   6          no (8)       yes, no crash
  Half-Life 2: DM    7          no (8)       yes, no crash

(Listeners counts SDK Hooks' own.) On x86_64, every one of these games
grows the C runtime's block. TF2's tier0 allocates from the Windows
process heap, as the C runtime does, so its realloc happens to work on
the block; Day of Infamy's and Insurgency's tier0 has heaps of its own, takes
the block for one of its own and crashes. On x86, the vector has room so far, but
the count isn't fixed: map entities like ai_relationship, ai_goal_*,
logic_navigation or ai_speechfilter add themselves while they're
active, as do other extensions, and HL2DM leaves one slot. One more
listener, and a 32-bit server grows the C runtime's block too.

Grow the vector with g_pMemAlloc->Realloc before adding the listener,
so its block is always tier0's.

To reproduce the crash, on Day of Infamy's 64-bit Windows server
(srcds_x64), load a plugin at startup that only includes <sdkhooks>
and does nothing: the server crashes at the map start. With full page
heap (gflags /p /enable srcds_x64.exe /full) the crash points at the
block's allocation in SDKHooks::SDK_OnLoad.

Tested on Windows 11 with SourceMod built with MSVC 14.52 (Visual
Studio 2026): the reproducer crashes Day of Infamy's and Insurgency's
64-bit servers with master at the map start, and Day of Infamy's runs
with this patch alone; Insurgency's 64-bit server grows the vector
without a crash with it. Loaded once the server is up, SDK Hooks
doesn't crash it, also across a map change. With the whole series, Day of Infamy's server runs with all of
SourceMod's extensions and 25 plugins loaded, on 64-bit (also under
full page heap) and on 32-bit.
---
 extensions/sdkhooks/extension.cpp | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/extensions/sdkhooks/extension.cpp b/extensions/sdkhooks/extension.cpp
index be521479..527130d6 100644
--- a/extensions/sdkhooks/extension.cpp
+++ b/extensions/sdkhooks/extension.cpp
@@ -126,6 +126,31 @@ IForward *g_pOnLevelInit = NULL;
 
 IGameConfig *g_pGameConf = NULL;
 
+// Adds a listener to the game's entity listeners, growing their vector with the game's allocator,
+// tier0's: CUtlVector grows with malloc and realloc, which the game's libraries route to tier0
+// (memoverride.cpp) but SourceMod's extensions don't. While the vector is empty or full, e.g. when the
+// extension loads at the server's start, the C runtime's block would be the vector's, which the game's
+// next growth hands to tier0's realloc: a block that allocator never made.
+void AddGameEntityListener(CUtlVector<IEntityListener *> *listeners, IEntityListener *listener)
+{
+	struct Access : CUtlVector<IEntityListener *>
+	{
+		static CUtlMemory<IEntityListener *> &Memory(CUtlVector<IEntityListener *> *vector)
+		{
+			return static_cast<Access *>(vector)->m_Memory;
+		}
+	};
+	CUtlMemory<IEntityListener *> &memory = Access::Memory(listeners);
+	if (listeners->Count() >= memory.NumAllocated())
+	{
+		int count = memory.NumAllocated() > 0 ? memory.NumAllocated() * 2 : 4;
+		IEntityListener **elements = memory.Detach();
+		elements = (IEntityListener **)g_pMemAlloc->Realloc(elements, count * sizeof(IEntityListener *));
+		memory.AssumeMemory(elements, count);
+	}
+	listeners->AddToTail(listener);
+}
+
 CUtlVector<IEntityListener *> *EntListeners()
 {
 	void *gEntList = gamehelpers->GetGlobalEntityList();
@@ -241,7 +266,7 @@ bool SDKHooks::SDK_OnLoad(char *error, size_t maxlength, bool late)
 	}
 	else
 	{
-		entListeners->AddToTail(this);
+		AddGameEntityListener(entListeners, this);
 	}
 
 
